<h2>How Often Does This Wiki Get It Right — Measuring Search and Graph Traversal</h2>
<p>This wiki introduces itself as a "RAG-ready knowledge source," opens a <a href="#!kgs-ai-layer">machine-readable layer</a> to AI, and ships its engine as a <a href="#!kgs-plugin">plugin</a>. Behind every one of those claims, <strong>there had never been a measured accuracy number.</strong> This page records two rounds of measuring. The first found the weak spots. After fixing them, the second measured again, adding fresh queries that were frozen before any fix was made. Good numbers, bad numbers and what the fixes cost are all here.</p>

<h3>Summary</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Round 1: search found words and missed sentences.</strong> Two or three content words almost always put the right page first, but all 20 Korean questions phrased as full sentences returned nothing.</li>
    <li><strong>Round 2: sentence search works now.</strong> On 40 fresh queries frozen before the engine was touched, hits in the top eight rose from 20% to 87.5%, and for Korean sentences alone from 0% to 93.8%. Empty result pages fell from 80% to 0%.</li>
    <li><strong>The related block is more accurate but leaves its folder less often.</strong> Dead-end edges fell from 34 to 4, lifting strict precision from 59.5% to 78.9%. In exchange, the share of cross-folder author links laid down within one hop fell from 19.3% to 14.8%, and three pages now have no related pages at all.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why measure now</h3>
<p>Two things happened the same week. On 23 September Anthropic widened Claude Marketplace into a catalog of about 2,000 connectors, plugins, products and implementation partners. It had launched on 6 March with six partners; enterprise customers can now spend existing commitments on third-party tools and receive a single invoice. Plugins have become <strong>products on a shelf</strong>.</p>
<p>The same day, Karpathy's LLM Wiki gist (the pattern in which an LLM builds and queries a markdown wiki directly, without a vector database) drew a comment asking whether anyone had benchmarked the claims about why it works. That week implementers started posting numbers. One measured precision over 1,536 queries and reported 0.3086 on its fast tier; another reported 94.0% on the long-term memory benchmark LongMemEval against 71.2% for the comparison.</p>
<p>Both point at the same moment. When a tool you built for yourself is handed to others, "it works" only carries weight once it becomes a number. This wiki is an instance of that pattern and ships its engine as a plugin. The question applies to it directly.</p>

<h3>What was measured, and how</h3>
<p><strong>Search ran the real code.</strong> A headless browser opened this wiki's <code>index.html</code>, waited for the knowledge index and the full-text body index to load, then called the same <code>searchDocs()</code> function the search box calls. It is not a re-implementation but the function the screen uses. It was measured separately on the Korean, English and Japanese screens.</p>
<p><strong>The query set was frozen before any results were seen.</strong> The first set has 107 queries of four kinds: one or two words typed into a search box, two or three content words with no particles, natural-language sentences, and questions whose answer spans two pages. Each query lists the page that answers it as the gold answer; where two pages qualify, hitting either counts. The queries were written by someone who knows the pages, so their vocabulary sits closer to the text than a real reader's would. The high content-word scores should be read as <strong>an upper bound</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Round 2 added a second set.</strong> The engine was fixed by studying where the first set failed, so any gain on that set is partly fitted to it. Before touching the engine, 48 fresh queries (32 sentences, 8 one-or-two-word queries, 8 two-page questions) were written and committed on their own. In round 2, the fresh set's numbers are the ones to trust. This page quotes query sentences verbatim, so it was taken out of the search pool while measuring.</p>
<p><strong>The graph was judged against lines authors drew.</strong> The "Related" block under each page (the same <code>related</code> an AI follows in <code>knowledge-graph.json</code>) is computed only from <a href="#!kgs-edges">the rarity of shared concepts</a>. Links authors wrote into the text, and the typed relations people declared, play no part in that calculation, so they make an independent yardstick. Edges backed by neither were judged one by one, by the AI that wrote this page and not by a person.</p>
<p>The tool, the sets and the judgments are all in the repository under <code>tools/eval/</code>: <code>retrieval.mjs</code> is the harness, <code>retrieval-set.json</code> and <code>retrieval-set-fresh.json</code> the queries, <code>edge-audit.json</code> the judgments. Anyone can re-run it and get the same numbers, or change a judgment and get different ones.</p>

