<p>Among the 20 Korean sentences, 17 contained a <strong>dead piece</strong> like this: a verb ending with a question mark attached, the Korean equivalent of "documents?". Dead pieces were not the whole story. On a Korean example question about when skills are loaded, removing its dead ending still left four pieces that no single page shared, so it stayed at zero. The more pieces a query has, the fewer pages hold all of them.</p>
<p>A what-if tested dropping only the dead pieces and searching again. English sentences rose from 10% to <strong>70%</strong> in the top eight, but Korean rose only from 0% to 15%, and Japanese from 10% to 20%. Japanese is written without spaces, so a whole sentence became a single piece and died as a unit. In Korean, pieces with particles attached scattered across different pages and their intersection came out empty.</p>
<p>Word search had one weakness too. Typing "harness" on the Korean screen put the defining page, <a href="#!hns-overview">What a Harness Is</a>, in <strong>sixth</strong> place. Eight pages carried "harness" as a concept, all eight tied at the top score of 8, and on a tie the newest page went first. Each new news piece on the topic pushed the defining page one place further down. In English, one query for "adoption" missed. The matching page is titled "Adopting Ontologies" and never uses "adoption" in its text, and without stemming the two words never met.</p>

