<p>Precision held up. Following an edge landed, seven times in eight, on a page that at least touched the topic. The 34 dead ends had three causes. Twenty came from <strong>concepts that are too common</strong>: when two words like plugin, install, skill or token cost overlapped, an edge formed even across unrelated topics. Rarity weighting does make common concepts count for less, but the rule let any pair sharing two or more concepts through, so two light ones together cleared the bar. Ten were <strong>homonyms</strong>. "Metadata" meaning a note's front matter and "metadata" meaning a database's metadata views were filed under one concept key, and "retry" in agent cost and "retry" in message delivery the same. The last four were <strong>folder fill</strong>, where a page short of shared concepts is topped up with folder neighbours; in a folder with scattered topics, like the news folder, those neighbours were unrelated.</p>
<p>Recall was the weak side. Of the links where an author wrote "see also this" into the text, the related block laid down a third within one hop, and a fifth for links that cross folders. Part of that is two lines measuring different things: concept edges follow what a page is about; body links follow what a passage brings to mind. Still, an AI that traverses only through the related block misses two thirds of the paths authors drew.</p>
<p>Joining the two showed the bottleneck. On 12 questions whose answer spans two pages, both pages landed in search's top three for only one. Yet for three of the four questions where search put at least one of the pair in its top three, the related block supplied the other. For the other 8, search missed both, and 7 of those returned nothing at all. <strong>The door into the graph was narrower than the graph.</strong></p>

<h3>What was fixed</h3>
<p><strong>Search · trimming pieces.</strong> Punctuation (fullwidth included) is stripped from each piece, and for Korean the forms left after cutting common endings and particles are searched too. A Korean question form of "connect" now matches if any of its trimmed forms does. A Latin word with a Korean particle glued on, such as "code" plus the particle for "in," is searched as "code." English gains a stem with suffixes like -ing, -ation and -s removed, so "adoption" reaches "Adopting" through "adopt." Japanese keeps only runs of katakana, kanji and Latin letters and drops hiragana (particles and endings), which cuts a sentence into words. Question words such as "how" and stand-alone question verbs are dropped. This uses lists, not a morphological analyzer, so it sometimes cuts in the wrong place.</p>
<p><strong>Search · three passes.</strong> (1) Pages holding every piece. (2) If none, drop pieces that match nowhere and try again. (3) If still none, list pages holding at least half of the remaining pieces, most pieces first (or any piece at all, if no page reaches half). Results from (2) and (3) carry a notice: "No page contains every word, so these match only some of them."</p>
<p><strong>Search · tie-breaking.</strong> A page whose title starts with the word gets 3 extra points, and 1 if the title merely contains it. If still tied, evergreen pages come before dated news.</p>
<p><strong>Graph · a higher bar across folders.</strong> Edges inside a folder follow the old rule (two or more shared concepts, or one rare one). Only edges that cross folders face a higher bar: three or more shared concepts, or two where at least one is rare (appearing in three pages or fewer). The goal is to stop edges that cleared on two common words. The criterion was fixed before choosing a rule: the highest precision without losing correct edges. Raising the bar everywhere gave similar precision but lost 11 backed edges. The full rule is in <a href="#!kgs-edges">How Documents Connect</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Graph · no folder fill for dated folders.</strong> Dated pieces like news are neither topped up with folder neighbours nor used to top up others.</p>
<p><strong>Graph · two unsupported concepts removed.</strong> Checked against the text, "security grade" on <a href="#!news-20260904-model-doors">Doors on the Model</a> and "responsibility boundary" on <a href="#!claude-md-constitution">Claude's Constitution</a> did not appear in their bodies, and both were creating homonym edges. They were removed. "Metadata" was a homonym too, but removing it would shift a boundary value and create new homonym edges elsewhere, so it stayed.</p>
<p><strong>Translation · relation evidence.</strong> During round 1, 13 evidence notes on typed relations turned out to show in Korean on the English and Japanese screens. All 13 were translated, and a gate was added so the validator fails if it happens again.</p>

