<p>How did sentence queries get their answers? Of 36 Korean sentences, 21 were answered in pass (2) or (3), as partial matches. For English it was 3 of 18, and for Japanese none of 18. Korean sentences break into many pieces, so a page holding all of them is rare. The notice is shown, but such a result deserves less trust than one where every word matched.</p>
<p>The remaining misses mostly fall into two kinds. One is asking in words the page does not use: the answer to a Korean question about running Claude Code "on a company server" never uses the word for "company" and uses "self-hosting" six times. The other is a page that matches more pieces ranking ahead: for a question about how many stages Second Brain has, the per-level pages came before the introduction. Cutting endings solves neither. That needs a synonym list or meaning-based search.</p>

