<p>There are 53 fewer edges but 7 more correct ones. Of the 73 edges that disappeared, 61 were weak or unrelated; of the 20 that appeared, 19 are backed or judged related. Most new edges are the previous and next parts of a series, supplied by folder fill. <a href="#!kgs-gates">How the Wiki Guards Itself</a>, for example, now links to its own series instead of a skills page it shared two common concepts with.</p>
<p>Of the two pairs still crossing Worlds, one is the "knowledge management" lineage between an LLM Wiki article and the DOUZONE knowledge platform proposal, a bridge that reflects a real relationship and rightly stays. The other (the Skills introduction and the proposal's security and roadmap page) is still judged unrelated. One of its two shared concepts is rare, so it cleared the new bar too.</p>
<p>The two-page questions were re-measured too. On the 12 in the first set, both pages in search's top three went from 8.3% to 25%, and with one hop of related blocks from 25% to 75%. Questions with a foothold (at least one of the pair in the top three) grew from 4 to 10, and the related block completed 7 of them. On the 8 fresh ones, the top three went from 0% to 50%, but one hop added nothing: for the 2 questions where search found only one page, the related block did not hold the other.</p>

<h3>What the fixes cost</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Twelve good edges.</strong> Among the 73 lost edges were 6 backed ones and 6 judged related. Cross-folder links like those between <a href="#!wr-knowledge-vault">the reflection on knowledge stores</a> and the Second Brain level pages, or between LLM Wiki onboarding and that reflection, rested on two concepts that are not rare or on one rare concept, and the new bar caught them. The bar sees only what two pages share, not whether the link is good. This is why cross-folder recall at one hop fell from 19.3% to 14.8%.</li>
    <li><strong>Three pages with an empty related block.</strong> They sit alone in their folders: the im-not-ai skill, the dews-sourcegraph skill and the notification engine proposal. The 8 edges they used to carry were 6 unrelated and 2 weak. Showing nothing seemed better than showing dead ends, but an empty block is still empty.</li>
    <li><strong>Four dead ends remain.</strong> The one World-crossing pair above (two directions) and two common-concept edges into the usage article.</li>
    <li><strong>Partial results.</strong> Empty pages are gone, but more than half of Korean sentence queries now return partial matches. That is also where a wrong page can end up on top.</li>
</ul>
<p>Vocabulary is next. Most remaining search misses come from readers and pages using different words, and the cross-folder links the graph lost are connections the concept lists do not show. Either fix can be measured with the same tool, and fresh queries will again be frozen before the change.</p>

<h3>Why these numbers are not set beside other implementations'</h3>
<p>It is tempting to line this page's figures up next to what LLM Wiki implementers posted (precision over 1,536 queries, accuracy on a long-term memory benchmark). This page does not. The tasks differ. They measured systems that store and recall memory on standard benchmarks; this page measured a published wiki's site search and related blocks on its own queries. One table would compare arrows shot at different targets. Their figures are also all self-reported.</p>
<p>The point here is not the size of the numbers but <strong>being on the side that publishes them</strong>. With the queries and judgments public, anyone can re-measure this wiki's claims the same way.</p>

<h3>Measurement conditions and fact-check note</h3>
<p class="scn-sub">Round 1 was measured on 24 September 2026 against the wiki at commit <code>fb6a831</code> (71 indexed pages, 74 searchable); search was run twice and gave identical numbers. Round 2 was measured the same day after the engine and graph fixes; because this page quotes the queries verbatim, it was removed from both the search pool and the graph counts (71 of 72 indexed pages). The 48 fresh queries were frozen in a separate commit (<code>ac66d57</code>) before the engine was changed. After the first run on the fresh set, one failure in the first set prompted two more tweaks (a few more question words, and a last "any piece" step in pass 3); the fresh set's numbers were the same before and after them. Both sets were written by someone who knows the pages, so scores may run higher than on real reader queries. The judgments on unbacked edges (165 in round 1, plus the edges new in round 2) were made by the AI that wrote this page, not by a person. The Claude Marketplace expansion (23 September, about 2,000 items, launched 6 March with six partners, purchases against existing commitments on a single invoice) was cross-checked with <a href="https://venturebeat.com/technology/anthropic-launches-claude-marketplace-giving-enterprises-access-to-claude" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VentureBeat</a>, <a href="https://thenextweb.com/news/anthropic-marketplace-claude-enterprise-software" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Next Web</a> and <a href="https://claude.com/platform/marketplace" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the official page</a>. The question on the LLM Wiki gist and the implementers' figures were confirmed in <a href="https://gist.github.com/karpathy/442a6bf555914893e9891c11519de94f" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the gist's own comments</a>; all figures are self-reported and no independent reproduction was found. The implementers are private individuals and are not named.</p>
